-- Insert a bond row that meets the condition bond_type = '#'
INSERT INTO BOND (bond_id, molecule_id, bond_type) VALUES ('b1', 'm1', '#');

/*
Now imagine that later the application (or a view) is extended so that a join is performed with a table that also has a bond_id column. For example, if MOLECULE is later altered to include a column named bond_id (perhaps holding some alternate identifier) and if one writes a query like:

  SELECT COUNT(bond_id) FROM BOND JOIN MOLECULE USING (molecule_id) WHERE bond_type = '#';

then in many SQL implementations the unqualified reference to bond_id in the SELECT list may resolve to MOLECULE.bond_id rather than BOND.bond_id. In that case Q1 (written as SELECT COUNT(T.bond_id) FROM BOND AS T WHERE T.bond_type = '#') will count the intended values, whereas Q2 may count a different set of values (possibly even all NULLs if MOLECULE.bond_id is NULL). This counterexample dataset (via the INSERTs above) thus shows how a later change to the schema can make Q1 and Q2 deliver different results.
*/

