-- Now consider these two queries where the comparison on block is written in two different orders:

-- Q1:
-- SELECT T2.language FROM sets AS T1
--   INNER JOIN set_translations AS T2 ON T1.code = T2.setCode
-- WHERE BAD_COMPARE(T1.block, 'Ravnica') AND T1.baseSetSize = 180;

-- Q2:
-- SELECT T2.language FROM sets AS T1
--   INNER JOIN set_translations AS T2 ON T1.code = T2.setCode
-- WHERE T1.baseSetSize = 180 AND BAD_COMPARE(T1.block, 'Ravnica');

-- In a SQL engine that does not guarantee safe short–circuit evaluation, Q1 may attempt to evaluate BAD_COMPARE(T1.block, 'Ravnica') first on the row and then error or filter it differently than Q2 (which first sees that T1.baseSetSize = 180 and depending on the query plan may avoid calling BAD_COMPARE on that row). Thus the two queries can return different results (or one may error while the other runs) even though the logical conditions appear identical.

