/*
Now imagine that due to a bug, merger or import process the system treats user records with DisplayName 'csgillespie' as representing the same person. A later stage of the processing uses the fact that either user record identifies the owner. Under that interpretation, one would expect only one post for that person. However, Q1 joins posts against users and because there are two distinct user rows (Id=1 and Id=2) with that DisplayName, if the join were written not to use the key (for example, joining on DisplayName instead of Id) then the one post would appear twice – one for each duplicate. (Note that in a properly normalized database this anomaly should not occur; nevertheless, many applications have legacy data where duplicate user entries exist even though their Ids differ.)

To simulate the anomaly in a test database (perhaps when constraints are not fully enforced), one might write the join as:

  SELECT COUNT(T1.Id)
  FROM posts AS T1
  INNER JOIN users AS T2 ON T2.DisplayName = 'csgillespie'
  WHERE T1.ParentId IS NULL;

which would return 2. In contrast, the IN–based query (Q2) does not multiply the post:

  SELECT COUNT(*)
  FROM posts
  WHERE OwnerUserId IN (SELECT Id FROM users WHERE DisplayName = 'csgillespie')
    AND ParentId IS NULL;

returns 1.

This demonstrates that when duplicate users exist for the same real–world entity, Q1 and Q2 are not equivalent.
*/

