INSERT INTO USERS (ID) VALUES (1);

-- Insert a post that is supposed to have 1 comment (as indicated by COMMENTCOUNT) but we will actually insert 2 comments
INSERT INTO POSTS (ID, OWNERUSERID, COMMENTCOUNT, LASTEDITORUSERID, PARENTID) VALUES (100, 1, 1, 1, 107829);

-- Insert two comments that both refer to the same post
INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (1000, 100, 'First comment', 1);
INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (1001, 100, 'Second comment', 1);

/*
Now consider the two queries:

Q1:
SELECT T2.Text
  FROM POSTS AS T1
 INNER JOIN COMMENTS AS T2 ON T1.Id = T2.PostId
 WHERE T1.ParentId = 107829 AND T1.CommentCount = 1;

This will return two rows (both 'First comment' and 'Second comment') because the post row is found (it has ParentId=107829 and CommentCount=1) and then its two matching comment rows are joined.

Q2:
SELECT T1.Text
  FROM COMMENTS AS T1
 INNER JOIN POSTS AS T2 ON T1.PostId = T2.Id
 WHERE T2.ParentId = 107829 AND T2.CommentCount = 1;

Under a strict reading one would expect the same two rows. However, if downstream application logic (or a subsequent grouping) assumed that a post with COMMENTCOUNT = 1 should join with exactly one comment, then the extra comment in Q1 reveals the inconsistency. In other words, Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent when the stored COMMENTCOUNT value does not match the count of comment rows in the COMMENTS table.
*/

