/*
Explanation of expected behavior:
  • Q1 (driving from USERS) will filter the USERS table and then join:
      It sees two users with DisplayName = 'DatEpicCoderGuyWhoPrograms' but only user with ID = 1 has a badge.
      So it produces one row: 'Epic Badge'.
  • Q2 (driving from BADGES) starts with the one badge row and then joins to USERS. Under normal circumstances the foreign key ensures that the badge with USERID = 1 matches only the one user with ID 1. However, if due to duplicate rows (for example, if the underlying system or view inadvertently contains both user rows for the same logical entity) then that badge row might join with both user rows and be returned twice.

Note: Under strict enforcement of the PK constraint (and if the join matches on unique Id), both queries would normally yield the same result. This counterexample illustrates how the two queries can differ if duplicate values in the filtering column (DisplayName) lead to unintended duplicate rows in Q2.
*/

