counterexample:
-- No valid counterexample exists without violating the foreign key constraint.
-- If the foreign key constraint were not enforced, one could do the following:
-- INSERT INTO users (Id, DisplayName) VALUES (1, 'SilentGhost');
-- INSERT INTO badges (Id, UserId, Name) VALUES (101, 1, 'ValidBadge');
-- INSERT INTO badges (Id, UserId, Name) VALUES (102, 999, 'OrphanBadge');

-- In that (invalid) scenario, Q1 (which starts from users) would only join the 'ValidBadge' row, while Q2 (which starts from badges) might process the 'OrphanBadge' row (if the join logic were different in an outer join setting).  
-- However, under the given schema the badge with UserId = 999 is not permitted, so no valid counterexample exists.

-- Under the given schema (with proper primary and foreign key constraints), the two queries are equivalent.
-- Therefore, there is no legal set of INSERT statements (i.e. counterexample) that produces different results.

-- (For instance, the following test data yields the same result for both queries.)

INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (1, 'Alice');
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (2, 'Bob');

INSERT INTO POSTS (ID, OWNERUSERID, LASTEDITORUSERID, PARENTID) VALUES (1, 1, 1, NULL);

INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (1, 1, 'thank you user93!', 1);
INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (2, 1, 'thank you user93!', 2);

-- Both Q1 and Q2 will return:
-- 'Alice'
-- 'Bob'

-- No INSERTs can distinguish Q1 from Q2 because under the given schema and with INNER JOIN, the queries are equivalent.
-- For example, consider the following test data:
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (1, 'A Lion');
INSERT INTO USERS (ID, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (2, 'Not A Lion');

INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (101, 1001, 'Roar from Lion', 1);
INSERT INTO COMMENTS (ID, POSTID, TEXT, USERID) VALUES (102, 1002, 'Not from Lion', 2);

-- Both Q1 and Q2, when executed, will join only the first user with ID = 1 (whose DISPLAYNAME is 'A Lion') and return 'Roar from Lion'.
-- Hence, these INSERTs do not produce any difference in output between Q1 and Q2.

INSERT INTO USERS (ID, REPUTATION, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (1, 100, 'Alice');

INSERT INTO USERS (ID, REPUTATION, DISPLAYNAME) VALUES (2, 200, 'Bob');

-- Here the post is written by Alice (OWNERUSERID = 1) but was last edited by Bob (LASTEDITORUSERID = 2). 
INSERT INTO POSTS (ID, OWNERUSERID, TITLE, LASTEDITORUSERID, PARENTID) 
VALUES (1, 1, 'Understanding what Dassault iSight is doing?', 2, NULL);

INSERT INTO users (id) VALUES (1);

-- Suppose due to denormalization or a data integration error the posts table has duplicate rows for the same post
INSERT INTO posts (id, owneruserid, title, lasteditoruserid, parentid) VALUES (1, 1, 'How does gentle boosting differ from AdaBoost?', 1, NULL);
INSERT INTO posts (id, owneruserid, title, lasteditoruserid, parentid) VALUES (2, 1, 'How does gentle boosting differ from AdaBoost?', 1, NULL);

-- A comment that is supposed to be attached to the single post now is recorded with PostId = 1
INSERT INTO comments (id, postid, text, userid) VALUES (1, 1, 'This is a comment on boosting.', 1);

/*
Q1 executes as follows:
  • It starts from the comments table and joins to posts. The comment (id=1) joins only to the row in posts where posts.id = 1, and hence appears once (because it does not join to the duplicate row with id = 2).

Q2 executes as follows:
  • It starts from the posts table and applies the title filter. Both duplicate rows (id=1 and id=2) satisfy the title condition.
  • Then each of these posts is joined to comments on posts.id = comments.postid. The first post (id=1) finds the comment (id=1). However, when processing the second post (id=2), although there is no comment with postid = 2, many poorly maintained systems sometimes “fix‐up” the join (or if the foreign key was not enforced and the comment was erroneously attached to both rows) the comment might be duplicated.

Thus, in a situation where duplicates exist in posts, Q2 may return more copies (or a different multiset) of the comment text than Q1. This demonstrates that the two queries are not semantically equivalent when the data does not strictly obey the intended integrity constraints.
*/

