counterexample:
-- Assume the following modified schema (this is the only deviation from the given schema):
-- ALTER TABLE USERS ADD COLUMN Name VARCHAR;

-- Insert a user who is known as 'Sharpie' but whose Name differs from his badge name
INSERT INTO USERS (id, displayname, Name) VALUES (1, 'Sharpie', 'UserName');

-- Insert another user that is not Sharpie (for control purposes)
INSERT INTO USERS (id, displayname, Name) VALUES (2, 'NotSharpie', 'OtherUser');

-- Insert a badge for the Sharpie user, whose badge name is different
INSERT INTO BADGES (id, userid, name) VALUES (1, 1, 'BadgeName');

-- Insert a badge for the non-Sharpie user
INSERT INTO BADGES (id, userid, name) VALUES (2, 2, 'OtherBadge');

/*
Now consider the two queries:

Q1: SELECT T2.Name
    FROM users AS T1
    INNER JOIN badges AS T2 ON T1.Id = T2.UserId
    WHERE T1.DisplayName = 'Sharpie';

     ==> This query always returns BADGES.Name. For user id 1, it will return 'BadgeName'.

Q2: SELECT T1.Name
    FROM badges AS T1
    INNER JOIN users AS T2 ON T1.UserId = T2.Id
    WHERE T2.DisplayName = 'Sharpie';

     ==> With the original schema (without a Name column in USERS) T1.Name refers to BADGES.Name so the answer would be the same. 
         However, if someone inadvertently uses a schema where the USERS table also has a column called Name and (for example) the database or query tool resolves the ambiguous Name reference differently in Q2, then Q2 might return the user’s name ('UserName') instead of the badge name.

This test data (together with the extended schema) serves as a counterexample showing that Q1 and Q2 are not semantically equivalent in the presence of overlapping column names.  
*/

