Running Q1:
  SELECT COUNT(T1.id) FROM superhero AS T1
  INNER JOIN publisher AS T2 ON T1.publisher_id = T2.id
  WHERE T2.publisher_name = 'Marvel Comics';

The join will produce two rows (because our single superhero appears twice via publisher 1 and publisher 2) so Q1 returns 2.

Running Q2:
  SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT T1.id) FROM superhero AS T1
  INNER JOIN publisher AS T2 ON T1.publisher_id = T2.id
  WHERE T2.publisher_name = 'Marvel Comics';

Since both rows have the same superhero id (100), Q2 returns 1.

Note: In a strictly normalized database these duplicate superhero rows would not be allowed. This counterexample illustrates the semantic difference between COUNT(t1.id) and COUNT(DISTINCT t1.id) in a situation where a join yields duplicate occurrences of an entity.
*/

