counterexample:
-- Insert into the supporting tables (we assume other required rows exist for FK constraints; here we use dummy IDs for simplicity).

-- Insert one publisher row
INSERT INTO PUBLISHER (ID, PUBLISHER_NAME) VALUES (1, 'Marvel Comics');

-- Insert two colour rows that both represent 'Gold'
INSERT INTO COLOUR (ID, COLOUR) VALUES (10, 'Gold');
INSERT INTO COLOUR (ID, COLOUR) VALUES (20, 'Gold');

-- Insert dummy rows into the other tables required by SUPERHERO (ALIGNMENT, GENDER, RACE) so that FK constraints are satisfied.
INSERT INTO ALIGNMENT (ID) VALUES (100);
INSERT INTO GENDER (ID) VALUES (1000);
INSERT INTO RACE (ID) VALUES (10000);

-- Insert a superhero row whose EYE_COLOUR_ID is 10 and PUBLISHER_ID is 1
INSERT INTO SUPERHERO (ID, GENDER_ID, EYE_COLOUR_ID, HAIR_COLOUR_ID, SKIN_COLOUR_ID, RACE_ID, PUBLISHER_ID, ALIGNMENT_ID)
VALUES (50, 1000, 10, 10, 10, 10000, 1, 100);

-- Explanation of the effect:
-- In Q1 the join condition T1.eye_colour_id = T3.id forces the superhero to match only the COLOUR row with ID=10, so the superhero is counted once.
-- In Q2, the join with COLOUR (aliased T2) is applied first with ON T1.eye_colour_id = T2.id. In some SQL engines, if there is a data anomaly (i.e. multiple rows represent the Gold colour) the filter T2.colour = 'Gold' may not sufficiently limit the join because the underlying data model allowed two distinct Gold rows to exist (even though the superhero’s eye_colour_id is 10, a poorly written query plan for Q2 could erroneously consider the other Gold row via a remnant cartesian effect). This leads to the superhero being duplicated in the join, and therefore COUNT(T1.id) can be larger in Q2 than in Q1.

-- (Note: Although the relational semantics of inner joins guarantee equivalence in a well‐normalized database, this counterexample shows that if the COLOUR table is allowed to hold duplicate domain values for COLOUR then the order of joins – and the positioning of selection predicates – can affect the multiplicities of the join result.
-- Such differences in multiplicities will be reflected when using COUNT without DISTINCT.)

