/* Now, when the queries are run:
   Q1’s FROM clause (which joins superhero, publisher and gender) will yield an empty set. Then COUNT(CASE WHEN T3.gender = 'Female' THEN 1 ELSE NULL END) returns 0 and COUNT(T1.id) returns 0. Many SQL engines will then produce 0 (or possibly signal a division‐by‐zero error, but the point is that the aggregate is defined to be 0).
   Q2’s SUM(CASE WHEN gender.gender = 'Female' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) is evaluated over an empty set and so yields NULL, while COUNT(superhero.id) is 0. As a result the overall expression evaluates to NULL.
   Thus the two queries produce different results under this counterexample. */

