/*
With the standard inner join, neither query will actually join the row with NULL circuitId (since NULL = 1 fails), so both queries return 1. However, if an application (or a non‐standard SQL engine) were to write the join in a way that preserves the race with a NULL circuitId in Q2 (or if the join condition were mistakenly written so that the NULL matches itself), then Q2 would count 2 while Q1 would count only 1. This demonstrates the potential pitfall: COUNT(T2.circuitId) ignores the row with a NULL even if the row were somehow present in the join result, whereas COUNT(*) does not. */

