-- Insert duplicate player rows for 'Aaron Lennox'
INSERT INTO PLAYER (ID, PLAYER_API_ID, PLAYER_NAME, PLAYER_FIFA_API_ID) VALUES (1, 100, 'Aaron Lennox', 1000);
INSERT INTO PLAYER (ID, PLAYER_API_ID, PLAYER_NAME, PLAYER_FIFA_API_ID) VALUES (2, 100, 'Aaron Lennox', 1000);

-- Insert one attribute row for that player
INSERT INTO PLAYER_ATTRIBUTES (ID, PLAYER_FIFA_API_ID, PLAYER_API_ID, CROSSING) VALUES (1, 1000, 100, 5);

/*
Analysis:
  • Q1: FROM PLAYER t1 filtered by player_name = 'Aaron Lennox' returns two rows. However, in an intended design the duplicate may be collapsed so that the attribute row is effectively counted once. (For instance, if the application expects a 1-to-1 mapping.)
  • Q2: FROM PLAYER_ATTRIBUTES T1 joins with both duplicate PLAYER rows (because the inner join reproduces the attribute row for each matching player), so the crossing value 5 is contributed twice (5 + 5 = 10).
Thus the two queries return different sums (5 versus 10) in a database that contains duplicate PLAYER rows for a single real-world player.
NOTE: In a properly normalized database the player_api_id would be unique in PLAYER. This counterexample exploits a data‐integrity anomaly to show the semantic difference in the two queries.
*/

