/* Then, insert a laboratory record where the DATE is stored incorrectly as '1992-10-21' although the intended test date was '1991-10-21'. */
INSERT INTO LABORATORY (ID, DATE, UA) VALUES (1, '1992-10-21', 8.4);

/*
Note: With these inserts, if one were to run Q1 exactly as written, the WHERE clause (T2.Date = '1991-10-21') would fail and no row would be returned, whereas Q2 always uses the literal 1991. Thus, if a user were expecting the age to be computed from the lab test date, these queries might not return the same result or even the same number of rows. This counterexample therefore illustrates the semantic difference between extracting the year from the stored date (which may be in error) and using a hard-coded constant.
*/

