/*
Now, consider these two LEFT JOIN queries (modified from the provided INNER JOIN versions) which behave differently:

-- Q1: The college filter is applied in the WHERE clause
SELECT COUNT(T1.member_id)
FROM MEMBER AS T1
LEFT JOIN MAJOR AS T2 ON T1.LINK_TO_MAJOR = T2.MAJOR_ID
WHERE T2.COLLEGE = 'College of Engineering';
-- This query returns 1 (only member 'A').

-- Q2: The college filter is applied in the JOIN (ON) condition
SELECT COUNT(T1.member_id)
FROM MEMBER AS T1
LEFT JOIN MAJOR AS T2 ON (T1.LINK_TO_MAJOR = T2.MAJOR_ID AND T2.COLLEGE = 'College of Engineering');
-- This query returns 3 because even members 'B' and 'C' appear (with T2 columns as NULL) and T1.member_id is non-null.

This counterexample shows that the two queries are not semantically equivalent once the placement of the predicate is changed. */

