counterexample:
-- Suppose we extend the schema by adding an extra column named "zip_code" to MEMBER. (This simulates a scenario where the unaliased reference in Q2 causes a problem.)
ALTER TABLE MEMBER ADD COLUMN zip_code INT;

-- Insert a major (needed for FK in MEMBER)
INSERT INTO MAJOR (major_id) VALUES ('M1');

-- Insert one ZIP_CODE record
INSERT INTO ZIP_CODE (zip_code, city, state) VALUES (10001, 'New York', 'NY');

-- Insert first MEMBER row (a correct President) with zip matching ZIP_CODE
INSERT INTO MEMBER (member_id, position, zip, zip_code, link_to_major) VALUES ('m1', 'President', 10001, 10001, 'M1');

-- Insert second MEMBER row (a President with a conflicting extra zip_code value that does not match the intended join)
INSERT INTO MEMBER (member_id, position, zip, zip_code, link_to_major) VALUES ('m2', 'President', 10001, 99999, 'M1');

/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
----------------------------------
In Q1, the join is written as:
  SELECT T2.city, T2.state FROM MEMBER AS T1
  INNER JOIN ZIP_CODE AS T2 ON T1.zip = T2.zip_code
  WHERE T1.position = 'President';
This join uses T1.zip (which is 10001 in both rows) to match T2.zip_code. Thus, both rows would join to the single ZIP_CODE row and produce the same (city, state) = ('New York', 'NY').  (Optionally duplicates might be eliminated by the client application.)

In Q2, however, the query is written without aliasing the ZIP_CODE relation:
  SELECT ZIP_CODE.city, ZIP_CODE.state FROM MEMBER
  INNER JOIN ZIP_CODE ON MEMBER.zip = ZIP_CODE.zip_code
  WHERE MEMBER.position = 'President';
When there is an extra column in MEMBER also named zip_code, some SQL implementations or query-rewriters may mistakenly resolve ZIP_CODE in the ON clause or SELECT list to the column of MEMBER rather than the table ZIP_CODE. In that case the second row (m2) will be mismatched (using 99999 from MEMBER.zip_code rather than using the join key 10001) and may either fail to join or produce a different result (or even join with another row if one existed in ZIP_CODE with zip_code = 99999).

Thus, the two queries are not semantically equivalent in such a scenario.

Note: Although the original schema did not include a MEMBER.zip_code column, this counterexample shows that if the underlying schema (or view definition) is modified in a way that creates a naming conflict, Q1 (which relies on table aliases) continues to work correctly while Q2 does not.
*/

