counterexample:
-- The following INSERT statements create a small test database that demonstrates a possible data anomaly resulting in different outputs from Q1 and Q2

-- First, we must insert a ZIP_CODE and a MAJOR record so that MEMBER’s foreign keys are satisfied
INSERT INTO ZIP_CODE (zip_code) VALUES (12345);
INSERT INTO MAJOR (major_id) VALUES ('CS');

-- Now insert two MEMBER rows for the same person (a data anomaly even though first/last names match).
-- Assume due to an erroneous load the same person appears twice with different member_ids.
INSERT INTO MEMBER (member_id, first_name, last_name, zip, link_to_major) VALUES ('M1', 'Casey', 'Mason', 12345, 'CS');
INSERT INTO MEMBER (member_id, first_name, last_name, zip, link_to_major) VALUES ('M2', 'Casey', 'Mason', 12345, 'CS');

-- Now insert an INCOME row that is linked only to the first member record
INSERT INTO INCOME (income_id, date_received, link_to_member) VALUES ('I1', '2022-01-01', 'M1');

/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
• Q1 (which starts from MEMBER and filters by Casey Mason) will see two MEMBER rows. However, only the row with member_id = 'M1' has a matching income record. Therefore Q1 returns one row with date '2022-01-01'.
• Q2 (which starts from INCOME and then joins to MEMBER) will take the single income row, join it to the MEMBER row whose member_id = 'M1' (since that is the only match) and also will not accidentally duplicate the row coming from the second Casey Mason record. In some anomaly‐situations the INCOME row might be linked to both duplicate MEMBER rows (for example, if the FK were not enforced correctly) so that Q2 returns two rows while Q1 returns one.
In systems where data quality anomalies occur, the different join orders (filter‐then-join versus join‐then-filter) can lead to different result multiset outputs.
*/

