counterexample:
-- Suppose for the sake of the counterexample that the primary key and foreign key constraints are not enforced in MAJOR (or have been temporarily disabled) so that duplicate business majors can exist. 

-- Insert two MAJOR rows that both denote a Business major (with different major_id values):
INSERT INTO major (major_id, major_name) VALUES ('BIZ1', 'Business');
INSERT INTO major (major_id, major_name) VALUES ('BIZ2', 'Business');

-- For the purpose of satisfying MEMBER.FK to MAJOR, assume the constraint is disabled or relaxed.
-- Insert two MEMBER rows that (even possibly represent the same real-world person) link to the two different MAJOR rows.
INSERT INTO zip_code (zip_code) VALUES (11111);

INSERT INTO member (member_id, position, zip, link_to_major) VALUES ('M1', 'Manager', 11111, 'BIZ1');
INSERT INTO member (member_id, position, zip, link_to_major) VALUES ('M2', 'Manager', 11111, 'BIZ2');

-- Now, Q1 will join via MAJOR. It will find two MAJOR rows whose major_name is 'Business'.
-- The MAJOR row with major_id 'BIZ1' joins with member 'M1'; the MAJOR row with major_id 'BIZ2' joins with member 'M2'.
-- So Q1 returns two rows: 'Manager' (from M1) and 'Manager' (from M2).

-- Q2, however, is driven by MEMBER. Each MEMBER row is joined with the unique MAJOR row indicated in its link_to_major field. Thus, it produces exactly one row per member (M1 and M2).

-- In many SQL systems duplicates are not eliminated by default. However, imagine instead that the application or a surrounding DISTINCT eliminated duplicate positions. In that case Q2 would yield just one row ('Manager') whereas Q1 (which joins twice) would yield two rows if duplicates are not removed. This difference in multiplicity illustrates that the two queries are not semantically equivalent when the intended one-to-many relationship is violated (or when duplicates are allowed).

