counterexample:
-- First, insert into MAJOR. (For the purpose of this counterexample we assume that duplicate rows are possible – for example, if constraints are not enforced.)

INSERT INTO major (major_id, major_name) VALUES ('EE', 'Elementary Education');
INSERT INTO major (major_id, major_name) VALUES ('EE', 'Elementary Education');

-- Insert a ZIP_CODE row because MEMBER depends on it
INSERT INTO zip_code (zip_code) VALUES (12345);

-- Insert one MEMBER whose link_to_major refers to the duplicate major
INSERT INTO member (member_id, last_name, phone, zip, link_to_major)
VALUES ('M1', 'Smith', '123-4567', 12345, 'EE');

/*
Now observe:
  • Q1:
     SELECT T1.last_name, T1.phone
     FROM member AS T1
     INNER JOIN major AS T2 ON T2.major_id = T1.link_to_major
     WHERE T2.major_name = 'Elementary Education';

     The inner join finds two matching rows from MAJOR for member M1 (because of the duplicate row) and returns two identical rows.

  • Q2:
     SELECT last_name, phone
     FROM member
     WHERE link_to_major IN (SELECT major_id FROM major WHERE major_name = 'Elementary Education');

     The subquery returns the set {'EE'} (duplicates eliminated automatically) so M1 appears only once.

Thus the two queries yield different row counts even though they return the same columns with the same values.*/

