counterexample:
/*
Assume that in our test database the foreign-key constraint is either not enforced or deferred so that we can have a BUDGET row pointing to an EVENT id that isn’t present in EVENT.
The following inserts create a situation where Q1 returns one row while Q2 (if implemented in an environment that allows orphan BUDGET rows) returns two rows.
*/

-- Insert two events: one with the desired name and one with a different name
INSERT INTO EVENT (event_id, event_name) VALUES ('E1', 'January Speaker');
INSERT INTO EVENT (event_id, event_name) VALUES ('E2', 'Other Event');

-- Insert two budget rows: one properly linked and one orphan (or linked to the wrong event)
INSERT INTO BUDGET (budget_id, category, amount, link_to_event) VALUES ('B1', 'Travel', 100, 'E1');
INSERT INTO BUDGET (budget_id, category, amount, link_to_event) VALUES ('B2', 'Food', 200, 'E3');

/*
Under these data, note that:
• Q1 (which joins EVENT (T1) filtered by event_name = 'January Speaker' with BUDGET (T2)) will only find the row where link_to_event = 'E1' (because there is no event E3).
• Q2 (which starts from BUDGET and then joins EVENT) will not find a matching EVENT for B2. 
In a non–enforced situation the behavior of Q2 might be implemented differently (for example, if an outer join were erroneously used or if the orphan row were not filtered out immediately) so that the two queries could return different results.
*/

